>> title: Gigantes Plaki, Instant Pot
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: beans, tomato, greek, vegetarian, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot gigantes, pressure cooker gigantes plaki, instant pot greek baked beans, gigandes plaki, giant baked beans, Greek baked beans
>> servings: 6
>> dish: gigantes-plaki
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> pairs-with: tzatziki, pita-bread
>> time: 13 hr 30 min
>> step.1: soak overnight 12 hr — the one soak the pot does not take away
>> step.2: cook at high pressure 20 min in plain water
>> step.3: wait out the natural release 20 min, drain
>> step.4: fry 10 min, then the tomato 10 min
>> step.5: fold the beans in, reduce lid off 15 min, feta over

Cover @dried gigante beans{1%lb}(450 g) with @cold water{10%cups}(2.4 L) by four inches and ~soak{12%hr}. Every other bean on this shelf goes in dry, and this one does not — that is a real decision and not an oversight. A gigante is half again the size of any bean in the rest of the collection, nobody publishes a tested from-dry figure for it, and a bean that size taken from bone dry to done in one sealed run bursts its skin. Plaki is a dish of whole beans in sauce; a pot of split ones is a different, worse dish. The soak is what the method still wants, so it stays.

Drain @&(~1)soaked beans{}, put them in the #Instant Pot{} with @water{6%cups}(1.4 L), lock the lid and ~pressure cook{20%min} at high pressure. Plain water, no salt and no tomato — the beans meet the sauce afterwards, exactly as they do in the oven version, because acid firms a skin and a gigante has a lot of skin to firm.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{20%min} until the pin drops, then drain. Do not vent it. Twenty minutes at pressure gets a soaked gigante to barely tender, which is where you want it — it has fifteen more minutes in the sauce and a bean that is soft now will be mush then.

Fry @yellow onion{1%large}(chopped), @carrot{1}(diced) and @garlic{4%cloves} in @extra-virgin olive oil{1/2%cup}(120 mL) in the emptied pot on Sauté ~fry{10%min}, then add @canned crushed tomatoes{28%oz}(800 g), @tomato paste{2%Tbs}, @dried oregano{1%tsp} and @kosher salt{2%tsp} and ~simmer{10%min}.

Fold @&(~2)beans{} into @&(~1)sauce{} and ~reduce{15%min} on Sauté with the lid off, stirring gently so the beans stay whole, until the sauce is thick enough to sit around them rather than run off. Then scatter @crumbled feta{4%oz}(115 g) and @flat-leaf parsley{1/4%cup} over. This is where the oven version wins and it is worth saying: an hour uncovered at 350°F browns the top and dries the edges of the sauce into something the pot cannot make. If you have the oven and the hour, tip the lot into a dish and use it — the beans are already cooked and the last hour is the only part pressure took away.
